cpufreq/amd-pstate: Accept a fixed Autonomous Selection Enable that matches

Without the CPPC CPUID bit, amd-pstate takes the shared-memory path,
which sets _CPC's Autonomous Selection Enable for the chosen mode. On the
Surface Laptop 4 (AMD, firmware 4.355.140) every CPU's _CPC gives that
entry as the integer 1, not a register, so cppc_set_auto_sel() returns
-EOPNOTSUPP. Each CPU logs "failed to set auto_sel, ret: -95", the driver
fails to register, and acpi-cpufreq runs instead.

Treat the refused write as success when the fixed value already holds
what the mode needs. Passive mode, which wants autonomous selection off,
is still refused, as it should be on firmware that is always autonomous.

Of the modes that want it on, only guided mode runs on this firmware.
Guided writes CPPC Enable (MSR C00102B1) and Desired, Min and Max
Performance (C00102B3), all functional fixed hardware that
cppc_set_perf() writes directly. Active mode also writes the Energy
Performance Preference, and cppc_set_epp_perf() writes FFH registers only
when the platform _OSC grants CPPC's flexible address space. This
firmware grants 0x14 of 0x002e7eff (PR3 and APEI), so active mode's first
EPP write returns -ENOTSUPP and its init fails all the same. The kernel
is booted with amd_pstate=guided.

A similar change (Akram Boulahia, 2026-10-08) was dropped after that
machine's firmware update made the entry writable, and its author noted
it didn't handle passive mode. This change checks the fixed value
against the mode, so passive mode is refused as before.

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c b/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/amd-pstate.c
@@ -507,7 +507,7 @@
 	struct cppc_perf_caps cppc_perf;
 	union perf_cached perf = READ_ONCE(cpudata->perf);
 	u64 numerator;
-	bool auto_sel;
+	bool auto_sel, want;
 
 	int ret = cppc_get_perf_caps(cpudata->cpu, &cppc_perf);
 	if (ret)
@@ -532,8 +532,16 @@
 		return 0;
 	}
 
-	ret = cppc_set_auto_sel(cpudata->cpu,
-			(cppc_state == AMD_PSTATE_PASSIVE) ? 0 : 1);
+	want = cppc_state != AMD_PSTATE_PASSIVE;
+	ret = cppc_set_auto_sel(cpudata->cpu, want);
+
+	/*
+	 * Firmware may give Autonomous Selection Enable as a constant rather
+	 * than a register. A refused write is harmless when the constant
+	 * already holds what the requested mode needs.
+	 */
+	if (ret == -EOPNOTSUPP && auto_sel == want)
+		return 0;
 
 	if (ret)
 		pr_warn("failed to set auto_sel, ret: %d\n", ret);
